WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:09.259
So we've talked quite a lot already about the idea of your different modules being found, discovered, compiled and then linked.

2
00:00:09.259 --> 00:00:16.059
The thing that does that is actually the Unreal build tool, which is very, very important to be aware of when you're on the C++ side of things.

3
00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:21.059
The Unreal build tool is entirely distinct from the actual Unreal executable.

4
00:00:21.059 --> 00:00:26.760
The most common way that you'd be interacting with it is whenever you generate project files, which I'll demonstrate in a little bit.

5
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:35.460
But it's also worth highlighting that this is used when you open up your Unreal editor and you try and package the game.

6
00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:42.060
It's an entirely separate XE that worries about the compilation, the linking and then the actual creation of builds.

7
00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:46.179
Be that an editor build, be that a shipping build or anything like that.

8
00:00:46.179 --> 00:00:56.240
So like I said, the most common way that you would be interacting with this day to day is when you are generating project files, which might not be a term you're familiar with yet.

9
00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:07.540
The way you typically do this is you go to your U project file, your U project, you go to your right click context menu and it's this generate Visual Studio project files.

10
00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:11.040
So this is something you need to do quite commonly and I'll explain when you would do that in a second.

11
00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:20.340
The other case which is commonly comes up is if you're switching engine versions, then automatically your project files get generated and generating those project files uses the Unreal build tool.

12
00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:25.439
Now the case in which you would typically want to generate project files is whenever you introduce a new class,

13
00:01:25.439 --> 00:01:31.040
you basically have to rerun the Unreal build tool to make sure that it discovers that new file that you found.

14
00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:37.439
So if you add a new class and it doesn't seem to be appearing in your solution, that's because you need to rerun your Unreal build tool.

15
00:01:37.439 --> 00:01:42.120
And the simplest way to do that is generate project files just to make you aware of that.

16
00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:46.719
So let's talk a little bit about what the steps for the actual Unreal build tool look like.

17
00:01:46.719 --> 00:01:50.819
First off, we pass a project in with a desired target.

18
00:01:50.819 --> 00:01:53.120
So your target platform and a configuration.

19
00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:58.120
So that's debug game or debug editor.

20
00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:03.920
And what the Unreal build tool would do then is look through all the target CS files for that project,

21
00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.020
find the right target, and then start looking up the modules required by that target.

22
00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:11.520
So this is what we had in our example.

23
00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:16.719
If we were trying to build the editor target, it's going to find the target CS that matches that.

24
00:02:16.719 --> 00:02:18.319
It's the target type editor.

25
00:02:18.319 --> 00:02:23.419
It's going to find this and then it's going to start looking through each of these modules.

26
00:02:23.419 --> 00:02:28.419
What it will then do is include all the modules that each of those modules depend on,

27
00:02:28.419 --> 00:02:32.319
either through your private or public dependencies.

28
00:02:32.319 --> 00:02:40.120
And then it will basically step through every header file in each of those modules and look for kind of Unreal class metadata.

29
00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:44.719
So we'll come to these in a second, but that's your kind of U properties, your U functions,

30
00:02:44.719 --> 00:02:49.520
and your U markup, U classes and things like that.

31
00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:53.919
The thing that actually does the passing of those headers is a separate tool called the Unreal header tool.

32
00:02:53.919 --> 00:02:57.319
So whenever anyone talks about the UHT, that's what that is.

33
00:02:57.319 --> 00:03:04.120
And it is actually the UHT that is then passing through all those files and kind of generating Unreal specific code.

34
00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:11.819
Whenever anyone talks about the, whenever you see includes for a, I don't know, actor.generated.h,

35
00:03:11.819 --> 00:03:14.620
that generated file, that is what the UHT is doing.

36
00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:19.919
It's generating code based off that Unreal markup.

37
00:03:19.919 --> 00:03:22.919
So then the Unreal build tool generates an action graph,

38
00:03:22.919 --> 00:03:29.520
which is essentially a graph of all the things that need compilation and all the things that need linking for that desired target.

39
00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:32.819
And then the action graph essentially gets executed.

40
00:03:32.819 --> 00:03:40.719
The nodes on this, the nodes on the action graph are essentially CL.XE or link.XE with parameters,

41
00:03:40.719 --> 00:03:43.819
and they get saved into a make file so that it doesn't need constantly redoing.

42
00:03:43.819 --> 00:03:46.020
We don't need to redo this every single time.

43
00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:51.520
And then it'll execute that build graph and it'll perform all those compilations and all those links

44
00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:56.219
and it will generate those OBJ files and finally link them all together into a XE,

45
00:03:56.219 --> 00:04:02.719
a DLL and you'll generate your PDB files for your debugging symbols.

46
00:04:02.719 --> 00:04:06.719
So realistically as engineers, we don't need to know a huge amount more than that.

47
00:04:06.719 --> 00:04:10.219
The Unreal build tool and the Unreal header tool are relatively complicated.

48
00:04:10.219 --> 00:04:14.020
All we need to know is that we need to rerun them whenever we introduce new classes

49
00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:17.819
and that it is really what's being used underneath the hood.

50
00:04:17.819 --> 00:04:20.819
Both of these tools are actually available inside the Unreal source.

51
00:04:20.819 --> 00:04:27.620
So if something seems to be going wrong with your build process or with your parsing process,

52
00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:31.420
you can actually debug them as long as you have your symbols downloaded.

53
00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:33.120
So you can actually dig into them.

54
00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:36.720
They're not black boxes, they're available in the source code,

55
00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.019
but they are entirely distinct from the rest of the engine.
